I have reviewed your current procedures, and they exactly match the buggy versions identified in the architectural review. While they might appear to be "working" during happy-path testing, they contain severe transactional risks when interacting with your Java code.
Here is exactly what is wrong with your current scripts and why they must be replaced:
 1. **The Table Lock Bug (NC-1):** Your PROCESS_JOURNAL_BATCH uses /*+ APPEND PARALLEL... */. The APPEND hint triggers a direct-path load, which places an exclusive lock on the table. If two users approve batches at the same time, the second user's process will crash with an ORA-12838 error.
 2. **The Spring Boot Transaction Killer (C-6 & H-9):** Both procedures contain explicit COMMIT; statements. Because your Java service uses @Transactional, Spring Boot thinks it is controlling the transaction. If your Java code fails *after* calling the procedure, Spring will try to issue a ROLLBACK, but it will fail because the procedure already committed the data directly to Oracle.
 3. **The HDFS Data Corruption Race (NC-2):** In PROCESS_JOURNAL_BATCH, you are opening the o_cursor *after* the COMMIT. If another user merges data into GL_BALANCE exactly at that millisecond, your cursor will pick up their balances too, sending corrupted data to your HDFS Data Lake.
Here are the fully corrected replacements. Run these in your Oracle SQL client to overwrite the dangerous versions.
### 1. The Fixed PROCESS_JOURNAL_BATCH
This version removes the APPEND hints, reorders the cursor to prevent dirty reads, and removes the explicit COMMIT so your Java @Transactional annotation can actually protect the data:
```sql
create or replace PROCEDURE PROCESS_JOURNAL_BATCH (
    p_batch_id      IN VARCHAR2,
    p_executor_id   IN VARCHAR2,
    p_remarks       IN VARCHAR2,
    p_status        IN VARCHAR2,
    o_cursor        OUT SYS_REFCURSOR
) AS
    v_missing_rates NUMBER;
BEGIN
    -- 0. PRE-CHECK: EXCHANGE RATE INTEGRITY
    SELECT COUNT(*) INTO v_missing_rates
    FROM (
        SELECT DISTINCT REQ_CURRENCY FROM JOURNAL_REQUEST
        WHERE BATCH_ID = p_batch_id AND REQ_STATUS = 'P'
    ) req
    LEFT JOIN CURRENCY_MASTER cm
        ON req.REQ_CURRENCY = cm.CURRENCY_CODE AND cm.FLAG = 1
    WHERE req.REQ_CURRENCY <> 'INR'
      AND (cm.CURRENCY_RATE IS NULL OR cm.CURRENCY_RATE <= 0);

    IF v_missing_rates > 0 THEN
        RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR(-20001, 'EXCHANGE_RATE_MISSING');
    END IF;

    -- 1. Insert GL_TRANSACTIONS (REMOVED APPEND hint — prevents DML conflict)
    INSERT /*+ PARALLEL(GL_TRANSACTIONS, 8) */ INTO GL_TRANSACTIONS (
        TRANSACTION_ID, BATCH_ID, JOURNAL_ID, TRANSACTION_DATE, POST_DATE,
        BRANCH_CODE, CURRENCY, CGL, NARRATION, DEBIT_AMOUNT, CREDIT_AMOUNT, SOURCE_FLAG
    )
    SELECT /*+ PARALLEL(JOURNAL_REQUEST, 8) */
        GL_TRANSACTIONS_SEQ.nextval, BATCH_ID, JOURNAL_ID,
        NVL(REQ_CSV_DATE, TRUNC(SYSDATE)), SYSTIMESTAMP,
        REQ_BRANCH_CODE, REQ_CURRENCY, REQ_CGL, REQ_NARRATION,
        CASE WHEN REQ_AMOUNT > 0 THEN REQ_AMOUNT ELSE 0 END,
        CASE WHEN REQ_AMOUNT < 0 THEN ABS(REQ_AMOUNT) ELSE 0 END,
        'J'
    FROM JOURNAL_REQUEST
    WHERE BATCH_ID = p_batch_id AND REQ_STATUS = 'P';

    -- 2. Insert MASTER_JOURNAL (REMOVED APPEND hint)
    INSERT /*+ PARALLEL(MASTER_JOURNAL, 8) */ INTO MASTER_JOURNAL (
        JOURNAL_ID, JOURNAL_AMT, AR_FLAG, BATCH_ID, JOURNAL_BRANCH,
        JOURNAL_CGL, COMMON_BATCH_REMARKS, CURRENCY, JOURNAL_ID_REF,
        JOURNAL_PDATE, PRODUCT_TYPE, REMARKS, TXN_TYPE
    )
    SELECT /*+ PARALLEL(JOURNAL_REQUEST, 8) */
        MASTER_JOURNAL_SEQ.nextval, ABS(REQ_AMOUNT), 'A', BATCH_ID,
        REQ_BRANCH_CODE, REQ_CGL, p_remarks, REQ_CURRENCY, JOURNAL_ID,
        NVL(REQ_CSV_DATE, TRUNC(SYSDATE)), REQ_PRODUCT, REQ_NARRATION,
        CASE WHEN REQ_AMOUNT > 0 THEN 'Debit'
             WHEN REQ_AMOUNT < 0 THEN 'Credit'
             ELSE 'Zero' END
    FROM JOURNAL_REQUEST
    WHERE BATCH_ID = p_batch_id AND REQ_STATUS = 'P';

    -- 3. Merge GL Balances
    MERGE INTO GL_BALANCE target
    USING (
        SELECT j.REQ_BRANCH_CODE, j.REQ_CURRENCY, j.REQ_CGL,
               NVL(j.REQ_CSV_DATE, TRUNC(SYSDATE)) as BAL_DATE,
               SUM(j.REQ_AMOUNT) as TXN_AMOUNT,
               CASE WHEN j.REQ_CURRENCY = 'INR' THEN 1 ELSE MAX(c.CURRENCY_RATE) END as EXCH_RATE
        FROM JOURNAL_REQUEST j
        LEFT JOIN CURRENCY_MASTER c ON j.REQ_CURRENCY = c.CURRENCY_CODE AND c.FLAG = 1
        WHERE j.BATCH_ID = p_batch_id AND j.REQ_STATUS = 'P'
        GROUP BY j.REQ_BRANCH_CODE, j.REQ_CURRENCY, j.REQ_CGL, j.REQ_CSV_DATE
    ) source
    ON (target.BRANCH_CODE = source.REQ_BRANCH_CODE
        AND target.CURRENCY = source.REQ_CURRENCY
        AND target.CGL = source.REQ_CGL
        AND target.BALANCE_DATE = source.BAL_DATE)
    WHEN MATCHED THEN
        UPDATE SET
            target.BALANCE = target.BALANCE + source.TXN_AMOUNT,
            target.INR_BALANCE = NVL(target.INR_BALANCE, 0) + (source.TXN_AMOUNT * source.EXCH_RATE)
    WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN
        INSERT (ID, BALANCE_DATE, BRANCH_CODE, CURRENCY, CGL, BALANCE, INR_BALANCE)
        VALUES (GL_BALANCE_SEQ.nextval, source.BAL_DATE, source.REQ_BRANCH_CODE,
                source.REQ_CURRENCY, source.REQ_CGL, source.TXN_AMOUNT,
                (source.TXN_AMOUNT * source.EXCH_RATE));

    -- 4. Capture sync data BEFORE status update (fixes cursor-after-commit race)
    -- Use cursor directly — no explicit COMMIT (let Spring manage tx)
    OPEN o_cursor FOR
    SELECT
        j.REQ_BRANCH_CODE AS BRANCH, j.REQ_CURRENCY AS CURRENCY, j.REQ_CGL AS CGL,
        NVL(j.REQ_CSV_DATE, TRUNC(SYSDATE)) AS BAL_DATE,
        g.BALANCE AS NEW_BALANCE, g.INR_BALANCE AS NEW_INR_BALANCE
    FROM (
        SELECT DISTINCT REQ_BRANCH_CODE, REQ_CURRENCY, REQ_CGL, REQ_CSV_DATE
        FROM JOURNAL_REQUEST WHERE BATCH_ID = p_batch_id AND REQ_STATUS = 'P'
    ) j
    JOIN GL_BALANCE g
        ON g.BRANCH_CODE = j.REQ_BRANCH_CODE
        AND g.CURRENCY = j.REQ_CURRENCY
        AND g.CGL = j.REQ_CGL
        AND g.BALANCE_DATE = NVL(j.REQ_CSV_DATE, TRUNC(SYSDATE));

    -- 5. Update status AFTER cursor capture
    UPDATE JOURNAL_REQUEST
    SET REQ_STATUS = p_status,
        EXECUTOR_ID = p_executor_id,
        EXECUTION_DATE = SYSTIMESTAMP,
        EXECUTOR_REMARKS = p_remarks
    WHERE BATCH_ID = p_batch_id AND REQ_STATUS = 'P';

    -- NO EXPLICIT COMMIT — Spring @Transactional manages this

EXCEPTION
    WHEN OTHERS THEN
        ROLLBACK;
        RAISE;
END;

```
### 2. The Fixed PURGE_JOURNAL_BATCH
This version removes the chunked loop loop so a database crash doesn't leave a batch half-deleted. It also adds a proper ownership verification wrap to ensure users cannot delete each other's data:
```sql
create or replace PROCEDURE PURGE_JOURNAL_BATCH(
    p_batch_id IN VARCHAR2,
    p_user_id  IN VARCHAR2
) AS
    v_status VARCHAR2(20);
    v_deleted NUMBER;
BEGIN
    -- 1. Security + Ownership Check
    BEGIN
        SELECT BATCH_STATUS INTO v_status
        FROM JOURNAL_BATCH_MASTER
        WHERE BATCH_ID = p_batch_id AND UPPER(TRIM(CREATOR_ID)) = UPPER(TRIM(p_user_id));
    EXCEPTION
        WHEN NO_DATA_FOUND THEN
            RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR(-20003, 'Batch not found or access denied.');
    END;

    IF v_status NOT IN ('PENDING', 'QUEUED', 'ERROR') THEN
        RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR(-20002, 'Cannot delete batch. Status is ' || v_status);
    END IF;

    -- 2. Delete all rows (no chunked commits — let Spring manage tx)
    DELETE FROM JOURNAL_REQUEST
    WHERE BATCH_ID = p_batch_id
      AND REQ_STATUS IN ('P', 'R');

    -- 3. Delete Master
    DELETE FROM JOURNAL_BATCH_MASTER WHERE BATCH_ID = p_batch_id;

    -- NO EXPLICIT COMMIT — Spring @Transactional handles it

EXCEPTION
    WHEN OTHERS THEN
        ROLLBACK;
        RAISE;
END;

```
